Ask for the GLSL this target implements

`gfx_opengl.cpp` picks its shader dialect at compile time. On this target the
non-Apple, non-GLES branch is taken, and everything it emits is GLSL 1.10-era -
`varying` inputs, `texture2D`, `gl_FragColor`. The only thing about it that is
not is the version directive, which says 130.

oops-gl refuses that, in its own words:

    only GLSL 1.10, 1.20 and ES 1.00 are implemented; this shader asks for another

So the number is wrong for this target and nothing else is. `gl2-probe`'s
`libultraship-dialect` arm is the measurement: it compiles, links and draws this
exact shape at 120 - two samplers, three varyings, an interpolated colour input
and the combiner's multiply-and-clamp - and reads back the expected pixel; and it
asserts separately that 130 is still refused.

**That second assertion is what retires this patch.** If oops-gl ever implements
GLSL 1.30, the arm fails on it, and whoever sees that should delete this file
rather than the assertion.

Only the fragment shader is touched. `BuildVsShader` already asks for
`#version 110` with `attribute` and `varying`, which is accepted as it stands.

120 rather than 110 because 1.20 is a superset and costs nothing here: it is what
the rest of the collection's shader work targets, and a combiner that grows an
implicit conversion should not need this patch edited again.

diff --git a/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp b/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp
--- a/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp
+++ b/libultraship/src/fast/backends/gfx_opengl.cpp
@@ -291,7 +291,7 @@
         { "texture", "texture" },
         { "vOutColor", "vOutColor" },
 #else
-        { "GLSL_VERSION", "#version 130" },
+        { "GLSL_VERSION", "#version 120" },
         { "attr", "varying" },
         { "opengles", false },
         { "core_opengl", false },
